    "evaluator_feedback": "[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe syntax error at line 209, column 42 is due to an invalid use of a named set comprehension {e: EmergencyTrigger | ...} inside a let-binding, and then attempting to reference the bound variable et. In Alloy, let-bindings can introduce a name for an expression, but you cannot refer to a variable (et) defined only inside the set comprehension in the let variable itself. The construct let et = {e: ...} | ... (some et => et.admins else none) does not bind 'et' as a single EmergencyTrigger instance, but as a set, and the use of 'et.endedAt' inside the comprehension is not well-defined in this context. The intention is to find the relevant EmergencyTrigger (if any) for the State s, but the expression is malformed. None of the previous failed attempts addressed this variable-binding issue.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nReplace the problematic let-comprehension with an explicit quantification, avoiding illegal variable scoping, and use an inline some-expression to select the intended EmergencyTrigger.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: In pred R1R2R3, locate the line:\n    s.emergencyAdmins = (let et = {e: EmergencyTrigger | e.session = s.session && TO/lte[e.at, s.t] && (no e.endedAt or TO/lt[s.t, et.endedAt])} | (some et => et.admins else none))\n- Step 2: Replace that line with the following expression, using an inline some-expression and explicit quantification:\n    s.emergencyAdmins = (some et: EmergencyTrigger | et.session = s.session && TO/lte[et.at, s.t] && (no et.endedAt or TO/lt[s.t, et.endedAt]) => {et: EmergencyTrigger | et.session = s.session && TO/lte[et.at, s.t] && (no et.endedAt or TO/lt[s.t, et.endedAt])}.admins else none)\n- Step 3: Alternatively, if the above is too complex, break it into two lines using let to bind the set, and then use a conditional expression:\n    let ets = {et: EmergencyTrigger | et.session = s.session && TO/lte[et.at, s.t] && (no et.endedAt or TO/lt[s.t, et.endedAt])} |\n        s.emergencyAdmins = (some ets => (one ets => lone ets.admins else none) else none)\n- Step 4: Ensure that you do not use a let-binding to bind a set and then reference an element variable inside the set definition (which is not allowed in Alloy 6).\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix eliminates the scoping error by ensuring all variable references are valid and using Alloy 6's conditional and quantification constructs correctly; it is distinct from prior attempts that only bracketed or rephrased the let-binding without fixing the underlying variable scoping issue.\n\n[LESSON]:\nWhen using let-bindings in Alloy, do not attempt to introduce a variable inside the bound set and reference it outside its legal scope; instead, use explicit quantification or conditional expressions to access elements of the set.",
